# Detector acquisition protocols, and which samples they run on.
# Read by run_measurement_info() in id8_common/plans/acquire/master_plan.py.
#
# Full reference -- every field, every detector mode, timing rules and worked
# examples -- lives in docs/running-measurements.md.
# Validate any edit with dry_run_measurement_info(check_hardware=True) before
# running it.
#
# A protocol takes one of two shapes:
#
#   detector: / mode:   one detector at a time.
#   detectors:          a LIST of detectors run in the same beam window. Every
#                       protocol below is this one.
#
# Both shapes work in either file since 2026-09-11, when parallel acquisition
# folded into master_plan.py. Add `check_hardware=True` to the dry run to check a
# parallel protocol's devices as well as its fields.

loop_order: sample_major

################ Change here to run measurements ################

runs:
 - name: att_series
   samples: [1]
  #  protocols: [att40, att20, att10, att5, att2, att1, att40_end]
   protocols: [att1, att2, att5]
   repeats: 1

###### Change here to change measurement conditions #######

# ATTENUATION LADDER -- seven steps, one fixed spot, three detectors.
#
#   att_level  40 -> 20 -> 10 -> 5 -> 2 -> 1 -> 40
#
# The last step repeats the first so the pair brackets the series: if the
# closing att40 does not reproduce the opening one, the spot changed under the
# beam (damage or drift) and everything in between is suspect.
#
# att40 and att40_end are identical apart from the name. They HAVE to be two
# separate protocol blocks -- check_duplicate_assignments() in master_plan.py
# rejects the same (sample, protocol) pair twice in one file, so listing att40
# twice in the protocols: list raises instead of running.
#
# The seven protocols are otherwise byte-identical apart from att_level, so the
# only variable across the series is the attenuation. There is no YAML anchor on
# purpose: a change to acq_time, num_frames or a qmap has to be made in all
# seven.
#
# Every one sets sample_move: yes and position_reset: yes against the 1 x 1 mesh
# in sample_info.yaml, so every step rewinds the mesh index to -1 and drives to
# the SAME single point before acquiring -- sample_1, huber.x -0.2010 /
# huber.y 21.3250. total_pts is 1, so point 0 IS that coordinate and all seven
# steps sample the same illuminated volume.
#
# sample_move: no would NOT do this. sample_mesh_move() returns immediately
# unless sample_move is yes, so the run would acquire at whatever position the
# stage happened to hold -- and position_reset would be a no-op too.
#
# 2026-09-13: sample_info.yaml sample_1 MUST be the 1 x 1 block for this to
# hold. It was left as the 21 x 21 G0222 mesh after that scan; against a
# 441-point mesh every step would have driven to point 0 of the GRID -- the
# corner at (-0.266, 21.27) -- rather than to the spot below.
#
# That spot is mesh point r00245 of the G0222 map: gold-free (Au 111 fit SNR
# 1.67 on lambda2M, nothing at q = 2.7182) and still carrying the second sample
# peak at q = 2.833 (2theta 21.20), strength 0.498 -- second only to r00224,
# which was passed over because a gold grain sits 5 um directly below it.
# Nearest gold-bearing point is 7.1 um away. See sample_info.yaml and
# analysis/mesh_acq/out/G0222_twopeak.csv.
#
# All three detectors acquire in ONE beam window at 1 s per frame, 3000 frames:
#
#   3000 s   = 50 min of beam per attenuation step
#   7 steps  = 5 h 50 min of beam total, plus arming and HDF writeout
#
# Run serially that would be 17 h 30 min. dry_run prints both numbers.
#
# The qmaps are the ones the G0202 and G0222 meshes used, so the q axes match
# the mesh analysis exactly -- eiger4m_qmap_Sq360_Dq3_4m.hdf is the reworked
# Eiger map.
#
# att_level 10 is in the series. It was dropped on 2026-09-11 after a stuck
# filter left attenuation_actual at 1.78 with the setpoint at 10 (E0160 ran at
# the wrong attenuation); Suresh fixed the filter set on 2026-09-12. The note in
# trio_measurement_info.yaml saying otherwise is stale.
#
# att() warns rather than raises when the blades miss the request (strict=False
# is the default and master_plan does not override it), so CHECK
# attenuation_actual at every step. att40 is the largest attenuation this filter
# set has been asked for in this experiment and is the most likely to miss.
#
# Folder names come from the attenuator READBACK, not att_level -- on an earlier
# series att_level 20 read back as 22, so expect a0022 rather than a0020. Each
# step still lands in its own folder. With Reg1 at 223 the seven steps take
# measurement numbers G0223..G0229, so the opening and closing att40 never
# collide.
#
# NO DETECTOR IS MOVED by this file. All three have allow_motion: false in
# device_position.yaml, and no leg has a motors: block. Position them yourself.
# setup_huber_for_multi()'s huber.delta/nu motion is still commented out in
# master_plan.py, so the leg geometry in the metadata may not describe the true
# beam path -- raw frames are unaffected.
protocols:

  att40:
    att_level: 40
    num_repeats: 1
    wait_time: 0
    sample_move: yes
    position_reset: yes
    detectors:
      - device: rigaku3M_epics
        mode: EPICS
        acq_time: 1
        num_frames: 3000
        qmap_file: rigaku3m_qmap_Sq360_Dq36_NoStreak_lin.hdf
        analysis_type: Multitau
        select_device: yes        # this leg owns the Aerotech translation stage
        start_timeout: 30         # it stages for a few seconds before reporting
        hdf_timeout: 600
      - device: eiger4M
        mode: Internal Series
        acq_time: 1
        num_frames: 3000
        qmap_file: eiger4m_qmap_Sq360_Dq3_4m.hdf
